  you know starts listening to me on hey  we're starting this new project  and matt hensley was my friend i grew up  with and he skated for vision  skateboards with me and he left vision  jumped on 8th street this new startup  company right yeah let me let me jump in  here i know  people hate it when i do this but to put  into perspective  paul peralta was the company that danny  was amateur for  and and tony hawk wrote for them like  all the biggest names it was the biggest  game in town  and then danny way like 13 years old  like  like all of a sudden decides i'm gonna  leave paul peralta i'm gonna quit  like the whole world is looking this  like are you [ __ ] crazy danny you're  gonna  quit pal peralta he says yep i'm gonna  quit paw patrol j i'm gonna go ride for  this company  that nobody's ever even heard of before  hasn't even been launched probably it's  called  h street they're like h street like what  even is it and why the  [ __ ] would you do that and uh and and  then  sure enough it ended up it seemed like  the most reckless move  that like career-wise that danny could  have made it ended up being  like the smartest decision ever because  at that time  h street just took over it was this this  it was yeah i mean there was like  i i had the chance to get on hc before i  got on powell  because mike planted those seeds and  when we flew home from vegas  on that flight he was like hey i'm  launching this new project with tony  magnuson  we got brian lottie mike carroll and  like and he was like pitching it and he  was like you know we're talking to matt  hensley and i'm like well okay those are  my friends i'd love to be on that  program but  the bones were yeah dude you can't like  yeah nobody's gonna shake that vision so  i i had that opportunity  and you know i jumped on on powell um  because because the  opportunity opened up for me and i chose  to ride for powell over h street  but about a year into it you know and  believe me dude i was so blown away that  i even ended up on the bones brigade  you know that video part you're talking  about the public domain video part like  stacy peralta showed up and filmed me  that day with his own camera you know  on real film like he pulled up and  filmed bucky and i that day and it was  like  i remember after the session i'm like  you know i didn't even know if i was on  pal yet like i went up there  kind of like in this transitional period  with nobody yet telling me  you're on the team or not so i shoot the  video you know we skate all day and then  i asked stacey parolt afterwards i'm  like so  am i getting on the team he's like what  do you mean you're already on dude and  i'm like  whoa i've been stressing this whole time  like did i skate well enough did i pull  this off  so it was the biggest dream come true  but after you know a year of it like  if you look at the pecking order of  people that were in line you know and i  understand like they have some  you know some some celebrity pros on the  team and a lot of good young kids on in  in the lineup you know start moving up  so like it just wasn't happening i guess  fast enough where i knew i could you  know i knew where i was at with my  skateboarding and i knew i could  i could take it to another level if i if  somebody got behind me  and pushed it further and so you know i  wasn't after a little while i wasn't  getting the things i needed to go to the  next level and mike  came you know like there was a contest  in houston texas  that was your first pro contest it was  my last amateur contest  to shut up and skate houston shut up and  skate on this in skate culture that was  like back in the day that was  like you know it was like the  underground grassroots backyard but  you know as a skate park but backyard  you know ramp style  contest mentality you know just chaos  punk rock  partying the whole deal but the you know  it was also  the one you wanted to win because it had  the most street cred  so as an amateur showing up at that  contest was everything to me and i knew  that was the contest i needed to win to  really put myself  on the map to turn pro right and i think  in the first eight street video shackle  me not they showed you winning that  contest  yeah and that was the day i started  writing a nate street board because the  story goes i  i got so desperate to go to that contest  in the ba and the bones were in  powell was like we're gonna send you and  the last second they go no we're  boycotting the contest and i'm like  boycotting what the [ __ ] does that mean  you're boycotting it what are you  boycotting i'm gonna get to this texas  promise i gotta win that you know i  gotta try and win this thing  yeah and they're like sorry we're not  going and i'm just like dude hensley  everybody's getting ready to get on  their flight  ortega all my friends i grew up to ride  for hp now my hometown friends so i'm  like feeling left out of my  my skate bubble like these are the  friends i skate with every day now  they're on a program they're doing  everything i want to be doing  so i started to get like pretty jealous  and you know it started eating me up so  i called mike up and i'm like  hey you know i know you flew me home  from the one event you didn't ask for  anything in return i appreciate that but  dude i need help getting to this houston  contest like whatever dude from me  like let me in i'm like so desperate  dude can you help me get to texas  he's like yeah we can we can make it  happen but  only if you ride for us dude  yeah dude

